#!/bin/sh
# Fast-forwards main to the validated commit, after the guards have held.
#
# A fast-forward and nothing else. A merge commit, a squash or a rebase each
# produce a commit that was never built, which leaves nothing to promote: the
# image exists for the validated commit and no other, and rebuilding produces a
# different artifact wearing the same name.
set -eu

# The head is given, not resolved again. Resolving it here as well as in pr-head
# meant two lookups with two failure modes, and the second one failed the run
# after all six guards had passed - on a repeated approval, where the whole
# point is to find the work already done.
TICKET="${1:?usage: merge-change <ticket> <head-sha>}"
head="${2:?usage: merge-change <ticket> <head-sha>}"
echo "merging ${head}"

git fetch -q origin main
# What main was before this merge, read before the push moves it. The release
# decision below needs it, and after the push it is gone.
base="$(git rev-parse origin/main)"
if git merge-base --is-ancestor "$head" origin/main; then
  # Two actors reaching the same transition is expected, so the second finds
  # the work done rather than doing it twice.
  echo "${head} is already on main - nothing to merge"
  echo "head=$head" >> "${GITHUB_OUTPUT:-/dev/stdout}"
  exit 0
fi

git push origin "${head}:main"
echo "main fast-forwarded to ${head}"
echo "head=$head" >> "${GITHUB_OUTPUT:-/dev/stdout}"

# The release is dispatched, not left to the push event. A push made with the
# code host's own workflow token does not raise workflow events - that is how it
# prevents recursion - so an automated merge cannot trigger a release by pushing.
# Discovered by watching a merge succeed and no release follow.
#
# Dispatching is better than the alternative of pushing with a personal token:
# the chain is visible in the run list rather than implied, and no additional
# credential exists to be rotated or leaked. It is bounded by the same thing
# that bounds the merge - one merge per ticket, and the guards refuse a second.
# But only for a commit that has something to deploy.
#
# This dispatch used to be unconditional, and that quietly contradicted the
# requirement it lives next to: commits touching only documentation, planning
# artifacts, pipeline wiring or configuration SHALL NOT release. The push
# trigger honours that list and merge guard 4 honours it - this did not, so a
# wiring-only change went to production, and the guard then refused the NEXT
# change with "unresolved rollback or a leaked release". Three readers of one
# list, one of them not reading it.
#
# The list comes from the trigger, through the same script the guard uses. A
# copy here would be wrong the moment a project excludes a path of its own.
TRIGGER_FILE="${RELEASE_WORKFLOW_FILE:-.github/workflows/on-main.yml}"
IGNORE="$("${ACTION_PATH:-$(dirname "$0")}/../merge-guards/deployable-excludes.py" "$TRIGGER_FILE")"

# Three outcomes, not two. `git diff --quiet` answers 0 for no difference and 1
# for a difference, and anything else is a failure to answer - which must not be
# read as "deployable", because that is the reading that releases a commit
# nobody could classify.
# shellcheck disable=SC2086
git diff --quiet "$base" "$head" -- . $IGNORE && deployable=no || deployable=$?
case "$deployable" in
  no)
    echo "no deployable path changed between ${base} and ${head} - not dispatching a release"
    echo "production keeps running what it was running, which is what guard 4 expects"
    ;;
  1)
    gh workflow run "${RELEASE_WORKFLOW:-on-main.yml}" --ref main
    echo "release dispatched for ${head}"
    ;;
  *)
    echo "::error::could not tell whether ${head} touches a deployable path (git exited ${deployable})." \
         "Refusing to guess: guessing yes releases a commit nobody classified, and guessing no" \
         "leaves production behind main with the next merge blocked."
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

gh issue comment "$TICKET" --body "$(printf '%s' "\
All six guards held, so \`${head}\` is now main's head — by fast-forward, which means the commit that was validated is the commit on main, unchanged. Nothing was rebuilt, and the artifact promoted to production is the one that passed staging.

The release was dispatched — a push made with the workflow token does not raise events, which is how the code host prevents recursion, so the release is asked for explicitly rather than implied. When it finishes, the observation window opens and stays open until it closes on its own — this ticket stays open for the duration, because a change that is released and not yet closed is precisely what "still under observation" means.")"
